 competence: not explicit.\n- Decision quality: can make correct decisions quickly => strong.\n- Attention: excellent attention skills => strong.\n- Crew cooperation: puts concern for others above everything => positive, but regarded as egocentric and difficulty communicating ideas => negative.\n- Communication: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric => concerns.\n- Responsibility: not explicit.\n- Adaptability: not willing to further education => low adaptability.\n\nD:\n- Reliability: not explicit.\n- Stress resilience: responds to unexpected events adequately => moderate.\n- Technical competence: solves problems extremely well => strong.\n- Decision quality: not explicit.\n- Attention: can concentrate very well => strong.\n- Crew cooperation: considered moody, loner => concerns.\n- Communication: not explicit but loner may affect.\n- Responsibility: takes responsibility seriously => strong.\n- Adaptability: not explicit.\n\nThus B seems strongest on reliability and stress, which are top criteria. Concerns about communication and crew cooperation are important but maybe manageable.\n\nC has strong decision and attention, but communication issues and egocentric.\n\nD has responsibility and problem solving, but crew cooperation concerns.\n\nA lacks many.\n\nThus likely B is best.\n\nBut we need to reference Agent 1's recommendation of B and add our own perspective, maybe bring up concerns about B's communication and crew cooperation, and compare with C's strengths.\n\nWe can say we still support B but we need to consider C's decision quality and attention. Or we could shift to C if we think communication issues are more severe.\n\nLet's weigh: For long-distance flights, stress resilience and reliability are crucial. B excels there. Communication issues could be mitigated with training. C's communication issues are also serious, plus egocentric and unwilling to further education. D's moody loner also problematic. A lacks reliability.\n\nThus I will support B, but